  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rapid Support Mechanism in Somalia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the collapse of the Somali central government in 1991, Somalia has suffered a highly complex protracted conflict characterized by changing political dynamics and power shifts. Although there have been improvements to the peace and security situation in Somalia since the formation of the Federal Government of Somalia in 2012 and more notably in recent years through government-led counter offensive, the overall situation remains fragile. In 2024, Somalia is expected to continue facing a precarious situation as it still grapples with a convergence of complex challenges, including the ongoing impacts of security instability, climate change and humanitarian crises. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) has established a Rapid Support Mechanism (RSM) to provide rapid humanitarian response through local and international partners to vulnerable communities in need in the priority districts identified by the Humanitarian Needs and Response Plan for Somalia. The assistance provided will focus on prioritized sectors and locations. The RSM provides timely and effective humanitarian assistance to the most vulnerable populations by increasing the capacity of local partners to provide humanitarian assistance, including humanitarian coordination, water, sanitation and hygiene, shelter and non-food items, protection, health, nutrition and multi-purpose cash assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
